55 s on single_solve\" flag was measured under load (load avg 8-15 from parallel workers; the harness cProfile totals 2.8 s, mostly thread waits on the AES/inflate pool + ~0.5 s imports) -- measured here at load ~8: solve.py 1.2 s wall unpinned / 1.3 s pinned to 2 CPUs on the 276-id single (276/276 match expected); tottime shows no hot spot (threaded zlib inflate 0.24 s, hcfg scans 0.09 s, sha256 0.08 s, the d23 VB.NET overlay HCFG adds nothing measurable). Full solver-test 49/49 green; slowest cases are earlier-day 5-7-set Mastery bundles (4.3 s wall at load 15, ~12 s CPU across the fork pool).\n\nVARIANT AXIS: (speed cycle, no code change, D24) the \"5.49 s on single_solve\" flag was measured under load (load avg ~7.4 at worker start; harness cProfile totals 3.7 s, mostly thread waits on the AES/inflate pool + ~1 s imports) -- measured here at load 5-6: solve.py 1.20 s wall (1.3 s user) x3 on the 288-id single (288/288 match expected); tottime shows no hot spot (threaded zlib inflate 0.27 s = the 67 MB d08 member whose sha256 is asked, hcfg scans 0.09 s, AES-CTR 0.06 s, sha256 0.07 s). The two d24 patch-diff jars (~4 KB each) add nothing measurable.\n  Full solver-test 51/51 green at load ~14 (single 2.2 s, slowest 5-set Mastery 5.1 s). `hctf solver-run` wall ~50 s is harness-side (hctf.solvers.dispatch/question_index over every solver's cases + difflib), not solve.py; it reported missing [] / problems [].\n\nVARIANT AXIS: (speed cycle, no code change, D25) the \"6.1 s on single_solve\" flag was measured under load (load avg ~4.7 at worker start, 12+ during the suite; harness cProfile of the same case totals 1.42 s, mostly thread waits on the AES/inflate pool + ~0.5 s imports) -- isolated: solve.py 0.80-0.90 s wall unpinned on the 300-id single, 0.90-1.01 s pinned to 2 CPUs, 0.92 s pinned to 1 CPU. Full solver-test 55/55 green at load ~12 (single 4.1 s, slowest 5.9 s, all under parallel contention).\n\nVARIANT AXIS: (speed cycle, D26) Mastery grew to 10 sets x 312 ids x 35 MB `delivery N: evidence.zip` -- every forked set called lib.common.container_by_sha, which re-read and sha256'd EVERY download until its match (~0.25-0.37 s CPU per set, O(sets^2) overall: 10 sets ~3.5 GB hashed). _solve_pairs now verifies only the name-matched file (find_artifact) against the contract's paired artifacts[].sha256 (_want_sha, same pairing rule) and falls back to container_by_sha only on a mismatch. 10-set auto-mastery-235e63bb 4.0-6.6 s -> 3.0 s wall at load 8 (2 procs: 6.5 s); single 1.4 s (312/312). Remaining per-set CPU (~0.9 s: 90 MB inflate, sha256 of every member, all asked) is inherent. No answer changed; full solver-test 56/56.\n\nVARIANT AXIS: (speed cycle, D27) Mastery grew to 11 sets x 324 ids x 36 MB `delivery N: evidence.zip` (auto-mastery-4d45c8fb) and timed out at 8 s in the 2-proc replay under suite load (alone: 3.0 s). Cut per-set CPU without changing any answer: (1) each pool job pickled the whole 2.7 MB contract -> jobs carry only {container: sha256} (_want_shas, same pairing), the contract stays a fork-inherited global for the container_by_sha fallback; (2) ELF section names from a struct parse of the section headers (elf_section_names, == pyelftools on all 729 ELF members of the corpus; pyelftools only on extended numbering / bounds trouble, .symtab only when symbols are asked); (3) hcfg() xor via bytes.translate + one crc32, HCFG search via bytes.find; (4) containers mmap'd (_MapIO for ZipFile) instead of a 36 MB read+copy; (5) content-addressed inflate cache across the sets one pool process solves (key (method, compressed len, size), hit only on a byte-equal decrypted stream, entries unused by the previous set dropped): the shared 5.7 MB d22 FSharp.Core.dll inflates once per process. Floor per set is the 67 MB d08 inflate + sha256 (every member's sha256 is asked; d08 differs per set from byte ~28 KB of the deflate stream, so no prefix reuse). Full solver-test 56/56; 4d45c8fb 5.9 s at load 8 in the concurrent suite (was timeout).\n\nVARIANT AXIS: (speed cycle, D28) the single (392 ids) was flagged at 7.65 s and four 10-11-set Mastery cases timed out at 8 s in the loaded replay; answers unchanged. Hot spot: pyelftools' per-symbol construct parse of .symtab (~1 s per set: symbol counts / FPC / GNAT / handler-symbol predicates, the d28 vtable_symbol scan, elf_facts symbols, and get_symbol_by_name('recover_code') for disassembly). New _fast_symnames(data, types=(2,), want=None): struct-parse the section headers + SHT_SYMTAB (fallback SHT_DYNSYM 11 when there is no .symtab) entries, names from the sh_link strtab, utf-8 'replace'; want=name -> [(st_value, st_size)]; None on malformed headers -> pyelftools fallback. Verified == pyelftools on all 1268 unique ELF members of the corpus (names) and 153 d01/d15/d17/d18 members (recover_code lookup). Single 1.9 s -> 1.4 s at load 7-9; Mastery sequential CPU is now ~1 s/set, of which zlib inflate + sha256 of every member (all asked) is the floor. Full solver-test 58/58 green.\n\n## Proposed lib additions\n- lib.common.container_by_sha: check the name-matched file first / cache path->sha across calls -- every Mastery solver that calls it per set re-hashes all N deliveries (quadratic in the set count).\n- lib.common / lib.solverrt: Mastery fork pools should pass a small per-set job, never the whole contract (ProcessPoolExecutor pickles each job: 2.7 MB x 11 sets); keep the contract a module global set before the fork.\n- lib.binary.elf_section_names(data): struct-parsed section-name list matching pyelftools iter_sections() (incl. the NULL \"\"), None -> fall back to pyelftools; ~10x cheaper per member.\n- lib.binary: `elf_symnames_fast(data, types=(2,), want=None)` (solve.py _fast_symnames) -- struct .symtab reader, == pyelftools names; ~50x faster on large symtabs.\n\n## D28 item 13: generic empty-field fallback (lib.binary.generic_field)\n- After every handler ran, each field still EMPTY (None / \"\" / handler exception) goes to `generic_answer()` ->\n  `lib.binary.generic_field(blob, suffix, question, member, group=, answer_format=)`, with the same member narrowing\n  as `answer()` (`_narrow`: qualifier tag, question-named member, D28 \"do not use X\"). A non-empty handler answer is\n  never replaced and the fallback is not even called for it. Flag `HCTF_BINARY_GENERIC_FIELDS=0` turns it off;\n  `HCTF_BINARY_GENERIC_BUDGET` (default 2.0 s per `_solve_pairs` call = per Mastery set) caps its wall time. A\n  summary line `[*] generic fallback: N empty field(s), M filled, T s` goes to stderr.\n- generic_field reads the GM's spec sentence (predicate / count / sorted-set sha256 / first-match symbol / overlay /\n  CLI header / JSON-array / section-string grammar) over the member's REAL format, then the field-name families,\n  then `register_generic` rules (prebuild \"generic field\" targets). The per-format rules are listed in lib/binary.py.\n- Generic-only replay of all 58 cases here (every handler disabled): 11075 GM-verified non-HCFG fields correct, 0\n  wrong, 251 unanswered (D1 `disassembly` / `recover_code` only). All 24 earlier-family fields this solver left empty\n  on the D28 single come out correct. Cost: 312 emptied fields over the 12-set Mastery took 0.16 s sequential (about\n  14 ms/set). Tests: tests/test_d28_binary_breadth.py.\n{\n  \"38d29712-eaaf-4a2c-b849-3af1318197a2\": {\n    \"d01_artifact_sha256_1\": \"a0d3aeddb90dccf579e0262704dc7d7c2828121d8cc988f085de169601737b9b\",\n    \"d01_analysis_elf_class\": \"ELF64\",\n    \"d01_analysis_machine\": \"x86-64\",\n    \"d01_analysis_section_names\": \"[\\\"\\\",\\\".interp\\\",\\\".note.gnu.property\\\",\\\".note.ABI-tag\\\",\\\".hash\\\",\\\".gnu.hash\\\",\\\".dynsym\\\",\\\".dynstr\\\",\\\".gnu.version\\\",\\\".gnu.version_r\\\",\\\".rela.dyn\\\",\\\".init\\\",\\\".text\\\",\\\".fini\\\",\\\".rodata\\\",\\\".huntress\\\",\\\".eh_frame_hdr\\\",\\\".eh_frame\\\",\\\".init_array\\\",\\\".fini_array\\\",\\\".dynamic\\\",\\\".got\\\",\\\".got.plt\\\