{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Hkx2qesD2swSHtTpB3qUBY","type":"tool_result","content":"# binary-swift-justice solver notes\n\nVARIANT AXIS: Mastery set count (2 on auto-mastery-d1a6613c, up to 5 x 31 MB on D8) -- main() now groups (answer_id, question) pairs with lib.common.group_by_set and solves each set in a fork ProcessPoolExecutor (sequential fallback on pool error, HCTF_SOLVER_PARALLEL=0 disables); each set's delivery is still paired by contract sha256 via container_by_sha. Output identical to the sequential loop on all 9 cases.\n\nVARIANT AXIS: speed only (no answer change) -- the evidence.zip is all WinZip AE-2 (method 99, AES-256, deflate) and its 67 MB d08 Swift ELF was inflated serially through pyzipper (pure-Python HMAC-SHA1 + AES); unzip() now reads AES members directly (PBKDF2 + AES-CTR LE counter, pw-verifier check, no HMAC) in a thread pool, largest first, decrypt||inflate||sha256 pipelined for members > 8 MB, and returns Futures that group_members() resolves only for the group asked (falls back to the pyzipper reader on any miss). single 1.2 s -> 0.8 s local.\n\nVARIANT AXIS: speed only (no answer change; the 9.74 s was the harness's contention-measured Mastery estimate, real single = 0.5-0.6 s, 5-set Mastery 1.7-1.9 s pinned to 2 CPUs) -- per-set CPU trimmed: AE-2 members (CRC 0) skip the 67 MB crc32 pass, multi-set runs inflate the big member in one shot instead of the 3-thread pipeline (+20% CPU, ~0 wall), and the lazy deps (capstone/pefile/Cryptodome/lib.common) import once in the parent before forking. Floor: stock zlib inflate of the 67 MB d08 Swift ELF = 0.2 s/set.\n\nVARIANT AXIS: speed only, no code change (D12 single re-flagged at 8.49 s = harness contention, load avg ~6) -- measured single 0.90 s wall pinned to 2 CPUs (in-process 0.65 s), 5-set Mastery 1.3 s wall / 5.8 s CPU; critical path = inflate of the 67 MB d08 Swift member (0.35 s, single deflate stream, sha256 of it is asked so it cannot be skipped) + AES 0.08 + sha 0.06 + HCFG scan 0.07; imports 0.25 s (capstone already lazy). All 17 cases pass.\n\nVARIANT AXIS: (speed cycle, no code change, D16) the \"3.04 s on single_solve\" flag was measured under load (load avg ~6.6; the harness cProfile of the same case ran in 0.05 s) -- isolated: single_solve 0.5 s wall (0.6 s pinned to 2 CPUs), all 31 cases <= 0.9 s unpinned, worst Mastery (5 x 31 MB deliveries) 1.6 s pinned to 2 CPUs; the floor is the required sha256 + inflate of ~150 MB (already threaded AES-CTR/zlib + forked per-set pool). Answers unchanged; full solver-test 31/31 pass.\n\n## Proposed lib additions\n- `lib.common.fast_aes_unzip(data, passwords)` -> [(name, Future[bytes])] + sha256 cache: the `_aes_member` / `_fast_unzip` pair in day09 binary-nimble solve.py (WinZip AE-x without pyzipper's pure-Python HMAC pass; zlib/pycryptodome/hashlib release the GIL so members inflate in parallel). Every lane whose evidence.zip carries a large member benefits.\nVARIANT AXIS: (speed cycle, no answer change) the \"8.21s on single_solve\" flag was CPU contention from parallel solver-tests, not the solver: isolated it runs 0.55-0.60 s wall on the single (taskset 2 vCPUs, ~0.75 s CPU) and 1.2 s on a 3-set Mastery; critical path is the d08 67 MB Swift member's AES-CTR||inflate||sha256 pipeline (done at ~0.37 s) + ~0.09 s of field analysis -- measure with `taskset -c 0,1 /usr/bin/time python3 solve.py ...`, not under a loaded solver-test.\n\nVARIANT AXIS: (speed cycle, no code change, D14) the \"4.11 s on single_solve\" flag was measured under load (load avg ~4.5) -- isolated and pinned to 2 CPUs: single 0.51 s wall (0.6 s unpinned; the harness cProfile itself shows 0.048 s for import), 5-set Mastery auto-mastery-7439276e 1.42 s wall pinned / 0.80 s unpinned; all 21 cases pass with every answer_id filled. Nothing to trim that the earlier cycles have not already trimmed.\n\nVARIANT AXIS: (speed cycle, no code change, D15) the \"4.84 s on single_solve\" flag was measured under load (load avg ~6.6, harness cProfile counted 1.1 s of imports and 2.9 s of thread waits) -- isolated and pinned to 2 CPUs: single 0.90 s wall (critical path = AES-CTR+inflate of the 67 MB d08 member ~0.2 s, HCFG scans 0.09 s, capstone import 0.07 s), 5-set Mastery auto-mastery-546ae191 2.6 s pinned / 1.4 s unpinned; all 23 cases pass with every answer_id filled (solver-test single 1.26 s under concurrent load).\n\n## D17 (decoder-ring) single\n- New re-asked earlier-day fields (all GM-accepted): d01 marker (.huntress string), recover_code (imm of recover_code), d02\n  mangled_score_symbol (_ZNK15DiscoveryEngine5scoreEPKc), typeinfo_symbol (_ZTI of the score class), d04 has_go_runtime,\n  d05 has_rust_mangled_symbol, d06 methods (Main-Class method-table names incl. <init>) / access_flags (class access_flags\n  decimal, 49), d07 method_count (incl. <init>/<clinit>, 7) / has_coroutine_runtime (kotlin/coroutines/ entries -> true).\n- hcfg_storage: `base64` GM-confirmed for base64-stored blobs (d08/09/11/13). RAW-stored (d12 FPC .data, d14 .hcfg section):\n  label UNRESOLVED -- `raw`, `section`, `.hcfg`-family combos, byte/array/binary/xor/blob rejected; see single_solve/HELP.md.\n\n## Proposed lib additions\n- lib/vocab.json: storage / hcfg_storage = \"base64\" for a base64 (SENGR...) stored HCFG (GM D17 d09/d08/d11/d13); \"raw\" REJECTED for raw-stored (D17 d12/d14).\n\nVARIANT AXIS: (speed cycle, no code change, D17) the \"3.31 s on single_solve\" flag was measured under load (load avg ~4.5; harness cProfile counted ~1.1 s of imports and ~2.4 s of thread waits) -- isolated: solve.py 0.8-0.9 s wall on the 204-id single (run_solver 0.87 s; tottime is 0.24 s threaded zlib inflate of the AES members + ~0.2 s imports), 1.1 s inside the full concurrent solver-test; all 31 cases green. `hctf solver-run` wall (~6.5 s) is CLI/harness startup under load, not the solver. d12/d14 `hcfg_storage` (raw-stored) stay intentionally empty (unresolved GM label), so the single outputs 202/204.\n\nVARIANT AXIS: (speed cycle, no code change, D20) the \"5.28 s on single_solve\" flag was measured under load (load avg ~10; the harness cProfile of the same case totals 2.2 s, mostly thread waits + imports) -- isolated: solve.py 0.61 s wall unpinned, 0.61-0.74 s pinned to 1-2 CPUs on the 240-id single; in-process answer() work is 0.28 s CPU once members are decoded, unzip 0.55 s CPU (67 MB d08 inflate ~0.16 s, pycryptodome AES-CTR 25 MB ~0.1 s, sha256 ~0.04 s, all GIL-free threads); full suite passes, Mastery 0.6-2.6 s. d20_analysis_hcfg_resource_size = RS20 u32le @ magic+8 (380 on the single, matches expected).\n\nVARIANT AXIS: (speed cycle, no code change, D21) the \"4.46 s on single_solve\" flag was measured under load (load avg ~7-12; harness cProfile of the same case totals 1.83 s, mostly thread waits on the AES/inflate pool + 0.39 s imports) -- isolated: solve.py 0.60-0.70 s wall unpinned, 0.93-1.1 s pinned to 2 CPUs on the 252-id single (252/252 match expected); no hot spot in tottime (threaded zlib inflate 0.26 s, hcfg scans 0.1 s, AES-CTR 0.09 s). Full suite 45/45 green, slowest 5-set Mastery 2.3 s inside the loaded concurrent solver-test. Nothing new to trim; the d21 loader/plugin (.cmxs) analysis adds no measurable cost.\n\nVARIANT AXIS: (speed cycle, no code change, D22) the \"3.47 s on single_solve\" flag was measured under load (load avg ~6-7.5; harness cProfile of the same case totals 1.9 s, mostly thread waits on the AES/inflate pool + imports) -- isolated: solve.py 0.60 s wall unpinned, 1.0 s pinned to 2 CPUs, 1.0-1.5 s on 1 CPU, on the 264-id single (264/264 match expected). No hot spot: hcfg() scans total 0.07 s (0.06 s is the 67 MB d08 member; the 5.7 MB bundled d22--FSharp.Core.dll scan is ~2 ms). FSharp.Core cannot be skipped: d22_artifact_sha256_3 is its sha256, and its decrypt is ~30 ms in a pool thread off the d08 critical path. Full suite 47/47 green, slowest case 2.7 s inside the loaded concurrent solver-test.\n\nVARIANT AXIS: (speed cycle, no code change, D23) the \"4.55 s on single_solve\" flag was measured under load (load avg 8-15 from parallel workers; the harness cProfile totals 2.8 s, mostly thread waits on the AES/inflate pool + ~0.5 s imports) -- measured here at load ~8: solve.py 1.2 s wall unpinned / 1.3 s pinned to 2 CPUs on the 276-id single (276/276 match expected); tottime shows no hot spot (threaded zlib inflate 0.24 s, hcfg scans 0.09 s, sha256 0.08 s, the d23 VB.NET overlay HCFG adds nothing measurable). Full solver-test 49/49 green; slowest cases are earlier-day 5-7-set Mastery bundles (4.3 s wall at load 15, ~12 s CPU across the fork pool).\n\nVARIANT AXIS: (speed cycle, no code change, D24) the \"5.49 s on single_solve\" flag was measured under load (load avg ~7.4 at worker start; harness cProfile totals 3.7 s, mostly thread waits on the AES/inflate pool + ~1 s imports) -- measured here at load 5-6: solve.py 1.20 s wall (1.3 s user) x3 on the 288-id single (288/288 match expected); tottime shows no hot spot (threaded zlib inflate 0.27 s = the 67 MB d08 member whose sha256 is asked, hcfg scans 0.09 s, AES-CTR 0.06 s, sha256 0.07 s). The two d24 patch-diff jars (~4 KB each) add nothing measurable.\n  Full solver-test 51/51 green at load ~14 (single 2.2 s, slowest 5-set Mastery 5.1 s). `hctf solver-run` wall ~50 s is harness-side (hctf.solvers.dispatch/question_index over every solver's cases + difflib), not solve.py; it reported missing [] / problems [].\n\nVARIANT AXIS: (speed cycle, no code change, D25) the \"6.1 s on single_solve\" flag was measured under load (load avg ~4.7 at worker start, 12+ during the suite; harness cProfile of the same case totals 1.42 s, mostly thread waits on the AES/inflate pool + ~0.5 s imports) -- isolated: solve.py 0.80-0.90 s wall unpinned on the 300-id single, 0.90-1.01 s pinned to 2 CPUs, 0.92 s pinned to 1 CPU. Full solver-test 55/55 green at load ~12 (single 4.1 s, slowest 5.9 s, all under parallel contention).\n\nVARIANT AXIS: (speed cycle, D26) Mastery grew to 10 sets x 312 ids x 35 MB `delivery N: evidence.zip` -- every forked set called lib.common.container_by_sha, which re-read and sha256'd EVERY download until its match (~0.25-0.37 s CPU per set, O(sets^2) overall: 10 sets ~3.5 GB hashed). _solve_pairs now verifies only the name-matched file (find_artifact) against the contract's paired artifacts[].sha256 (_want_sha, same pairing rule) and falls back to container_by_sha only on a mismatch. 10-set auto-mastery-235e63bb 4.0-6.6 s -> 3.0 s wall at load 8 (2 procs: 6.5 s); single 1.4 s (312/312). Remaining per-set CPU (~0.9 s: 90 MB inflate, sha256 of every member, all asked) is inherent. No answer changed; full solver-test 56/56.\n\nVARIANT AXIS: (speed cycle, D27) Mastery grew to 11 sets x 324 ids x 36 MB `delivery N: evidence.zip` (auto-mastery-4d45c8fb) and timed out at 8 s in the 2-proc replay under suite load (alone: 3.0 s). Cut per-set CPU without changing any answer: (1) each pool job pickled the whole 2.7 MB contract -> jobs carry only {container: sha256} (_want_shas, same pairing), the contract stays a fork-inherited global for the container_by_sha fallback; (2) ELF section names from a struct parse of the section headers (elf_section_names, == pyelftools on all 729 ELF members of the corpus; pyelftools only on extended numbering / bounds trouble, .symtab only when symbols are asked); (3) hcfg() xor via bytes.translate + one crc32, HCFG search via bytes.find; (4) containers mmap'd (_MapIO for ZipFile) instead of a 36 MB read+copy; (5) content-addressed inflate cache across the sets one pool process solves (key (method, compressed len, size), hit only on a byte-equal decrypted stream, entries unused by the previous set dropped): the shared 5.7 MB d22 FSharp.Core.dll inflates once per process. Floor per set is the 67 MB d08 inflate + sha256 (every member's sha256 is asked; d08 differs per set from byte ~28 KB of the deflate stream, so no prefix reuse). Full solver-test 56/56; 4d45c8fb 5.9 s at load 8 in the concurrent suite (was timeout).\n\nVARIANT AXIS: (speed cycle, D28) the single (392 ids) was flagged at 7.65 s and four 10-11-set Mastery cases timed out at 8 s in the loaded replay; answers unchanged. Hot spot: pyelftools' per-symbol construct parse of .symtab (~1 s per set: symbol counts / FPC / GNAT / handler-symbol predicates, the d28 vtable_symbol scan, elf_facts symbols, and get_symbol_by_name('recover_code') for disassembly). New _fast_symnames(data, types=(2,), want=None): struct-parse the section headers + SHT_SYMTAB (fallback SHT_DYNSYM 11 when there is no .symtab) entries, names from the sh_link strtab, utf-8 'replace'; want=name -> [(st_value, st_size)]; None on malformed headers -> pyelftools fallback. Verified == pyelftools on all 1268 unique ELF members of the corpus (names) and 153 d01/d15/d17/d18 members (recover_code lookup). Single 1.9 s -> 1.4 s at load 7-9; Mastery sequential CPU is now ~1 s/set, of which zlib inflate + sha256 of every member (all asked) is the floor. Full solver-test 58/58 green.\n\n## Proposed lib additions\n- lib.common.container_by_sha: check the name-matched file first / cache path->sha across calls -- every Mastery solver that calls it per set re-hashes all N deliveries (quadratic in the set count).\n- lib.common / lib.solverrt: Mastery fork pools should pass a small per-set job, never the whole contract (ProcessPoolExecutor pickles each job: 2.7 MB x 11 sets); keep the contract a module global set before the fork.\n- lib.binary.elf_section_names(data): struct-parsed section-name list matching pyelftools iter_sections() (incl. the NULL \"\"), None -> fall back to pyelftools; ~10x cheaper per member.\n- lib.binary: `elf_symnames_fast(data, types=(2,), want=None)` (solve.py _fast_symnames) -- struct .symtab reader, == pyelftools names; ~50x faster on large symtabs.\n\n## D28 item 13: generic empty-field fallback (lib.binary.generic_field)\n- After every handler ran, each field still EMPTY (None / \"\" / handler exception) goes to `generic_answer()` ->\n  `lib.binary.generic_field(blob, suffix, question, member, group=, answer_format=)`, with the same member narrowing\n  as `answer()` (`_narrow`: qualifier tag, question-named member, D28 \"do not use X\"). A non-empty handler answer is\n  never replaced and the fallback is not even called for it. Flag `HCTF_BINARY_GENERIC_FIELDS=0` turns it off;\n  `HCTF_BINARY_GENERIC_BUDGET` (default 2.0 s per `_solve_pairs` call = per Mastery set) caps its wall time. A\n  summary line `[*] generic fallback: N empty field(s), M filled, T s` goes to stderr.\n- generic_field reads the GM's spec sentence (predicate / count / sorted-set sha256 / first-match symbol / overlay /\n  CLI header / JSON-array / section-string grammar) over the member's REAL format, then the field-name families,\n  then `register_generic` rules (prebuild \"generic field\" targets). The per-format rules are listed in lib/binary.py.\n- Generic-only replay of all 58 cases here (every handler disabled): 11075 GM-verified non-HCFG fields correct, 0\n  wrong, 251 unanswered (D1 `disassembly` / `recover_code` only). All 24 earlier-family fields this solver left empty\n  on the D28 single come out correct. Cost: 312 emptied fields over the 12-set Mastery took 0.16 s sequential (about\n  14 ms/set). Tests: tests/test_d28_binary_breadth.py.\n----\n{\n  \"38d29712-eaaf-4a2c-b849-3af1318197a2\": {\n    \"d01_artifact_sha256_1\": \"a0d3aeddb90dccf579e0262704dc7d7c2828121d8cc988f085de169601737b9b\",\n    \"d01_analysis_elf_class\": \"ELF64\",\n    \"d01_analysis_machine\": \"x86-64\",\n    \"d01_analysis_section_names\": \"[\\\"\\\",\\\".interp\\\",\\\".note.gnu.property\\\",\\\".note.ABI-tag\\\",\\\".hash\\\",\\\".gnu.hash\\\",\\\".dynsym\\\",\\\".dynstr\\\",\\\".gnu.version\\\",\\\".gnu.version_r\\\",\\\".rela.dyn\\\",\\\".init\\\",\\\".text\\\",\\\".fini\\\",\\\".rodata\\\",\\\".huntress\\\",\\\".eh_frame_hdr\\\",\\\".eh_frame\\\",\\\".init_array\\\",\